{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Only take heed to yourself, and guard your soul greatly, lest you forget, etc.</b> 'Take heed to yourself' means guarding the body, and it does not mention 'greatly' in it as it does regarding guarding the soul, for in guarding the soul a person must be far more careful than in guarding the body; therefore it says: 'and guard your soul greatly.'" "Another interpretation: these two guardings correspond to positive commandments and negative commandments, through which all the limbs and sinews upon which the human house is established are guarded. For our Rabbis of blessed memory already said (Zohar Vayishlach 170b) that 248 positive commandments correspond to 248 limbs, and 365 negative commandments correspond to 365 sinews in a person. For concerning this it was said (Ecclesiastes 12:13): 'Fear Elohim' — this is negative commandments; 'and keep His commandments' — this is positive commandments; 'for this is the whole person' — according to the number of his limbs and sinews. And they said further: 'all the days of the wicked he is חָלָל' — that is, dead as a corpse, for with every transgression he blemishes one of his limbs or one of his sinews. And it appears to me to draw a distinction between the term חָלָל (slain) and the term מֵת (dead), with that same distinction that exists between positive commandments and negative commandments: for מֵת — from itself implies [death by natural causes], whereas חָלָל means one killed by [human] hands. And one who transgresses a positive commandment transgresses through sitting and not doing — he blemishes one limb and it becomes dead on its own; whereas one who transgresses a negative commandment through rising and doing — it is as though he kills with his own hands some sinew among his sinews and makes it a חָלָל, emptied of the blood within it, and the blood is the soul. And its nullification even of one sinew is more dangerous than the death of one limb, and therefore the Torah was stricter in the punishment of one who transgresses a negative commandment than a positive one, for blemishing the sinews is more dangerous than blemishing a limb. And concerning this it says: 'Only take heed to yourself' — this is the guarding of his limbs, for they are a person's very essence, and their guarding depends on guarding the positive commandments. 'And guard your soul greatly' — this is the guarding of the sinews, for the blood is the soul and requires great guarding; therefore 'greatly' is stated, and this guarding is through negative commandments. And this is a correct interpretation." "<b>Lest you forget the things, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory (Avot 3:10) learned from here that one who forgets a single thing from his Mishnah is liable with his soul, for he causes that HaShem forget him too, as it is written (Hosea 4:6): '״וַתִּשְׁכַּח תּוֹרַת אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֶשְׁכַּח בָּנֶיךָ גַּם אָנִי״' ('you have forgotten the Torah of your Elohim, I will also forget your children'). Our Rabbis of blessed memory further derived (Chagigah 9b) from the verse: '״וְשַׁבְתֶּם וּרְאִיתֶם בֵּין צַדִּיק לְרָשָׁע בֵּין עֹבֵד אֱלֹהִים לַאֲשֶׁר לֹא עֲבָדוֹ״' (Malachi 3:18), that one who reviews his chapter one hundred times is not like one who reviews his chapter one hundred and one times. And I heard a hint for this: 'לֹא עֲבָדוֹ' ('did not serve Him') — its initial letters sum to one hundred, to say that one who learns one hundred times is still called 'did not serve Him'; 'עֹבֵד אֱלֹהִים' ('serves Elohim') to one whose initial letters sum to one hundred and one, for one who reviews his chapter one hundred and one times is called 'עֹבֵד אֱלֹהִים.'" "And this is my parable to make a precious hint from this, namely: when you examine the word זָכַר ('remember') and the word שָׁכַח ('forget'), you will find the number one hundred and one between them, and one who wishes to nullify forgetting should review his learning one hundred and one times, and thereby diminish from the number of שָׁכַח the number one hundred and one, and there will remain of שָׁכַח only the letters of the word זָכַר. Therefore, from then on, the matter will be hidden with him for remembrance and he will forget no more. And it is plausible to hear that the angel appointed over remembrance — זָכוֹר is his name, and he has רכ״ז (207) powers; and the angel appointed over forgetting — שָׁכַח is his name, and he has שכ״ח (308) powers; it turns out that he has one hundred and one powers more than the angel of remembrance, and therefore forgetting overpowers a person. Therefore a person needs to review his learning one hundred and one times, for with each and every review he diminishes and weakens one of his powers, and when he has reviewed one hundred and one times he has weakened all his extra powers and entered under the authority of the angel appointed over remembrance and will forget no more. A hint for this: 'הִשָּׁמֶר וּשְׁמֹר נַפְשְׁךָ מְאֹד' — the initial letters of these four words sum to the number one hundred and one; and the initial letters of the word 'לְךָ' spell לָמֶ\"ד, as if to say: לְמֹד תּוֹרָתְךָ מֵאָה וְאַחַת פְּעָמִים ('learn your Torah one hundred and one times') פֶּן תִּשְׁכַּח אֶת הַדְּבָרִים ('lest you forget the things'). And this is a precious hint."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {2 "לֹא עֲבָדוֹ: ל(30) + ע(70) = 100; עֹבֵד אֱלֹהִים: ע(70) + א(1) + א(1) + ל(30) = 102, but the Kli Yakar presumably intends the initial letters of 'עֹבֵד אֱלֹהִים לַאֲשֶׁר', i.e. ע(70)+א(1)+ל(30)=101.", 3 "הִשָּׁמֶר וּשְׁמֹר נַפְשְׁךָ מְאֹד: ה(5)+ו(6)+נ(50)+מ(40)=101. זָכַר=227, שָׁכַח=328; difference = 101, and שָׁכַח(328) − 101 = זָכַר(227). לְךָ: the letters ל and ך (final form read as נ=50) spell לָמֶ\"ד = ל(30)+מ(40)+ד(4) = 74, but the Kli Yakar's point is simply that the initial letter לְ of לְךָ sounds like לְמֹד ('learn')."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:01.230931572Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}